            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial upper bound\n\nOnly \\(n\\) lines are allowed, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering \\(S_n\\) with at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\) sunny lines  \n\nConsider the family of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b-a=i-1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b-a=i-1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+2-i\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n1\\le a\\le \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(L_i\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, and the union of the lines (2) covers **all** points of \\(S_n\\): every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), and the integer \\(i=b-a+1\\) lies between \\(1\\) and \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\).\n\nConsequently there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) that uses at most  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le n\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines (the lines in (2)).  Denote this covering by \\(\\mathcal C_0\\); it consists of distinct sunny lines and possibly some non‑sunny lines (if we wish, we may add any non‑sunny lines that intersect \\(S_n\\) – they are not needed for the argument).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and exactly \\(n\\) total lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(k\\ge m\\).*  \nTake the covering \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) (which already uses at most \\(m\\le k\\) sunny lines).  \nIf \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) contains fewer than \\(k\\) sunny lines, add further sunny lines that **do not intersect** \\(S_n\\); for example  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (n+10),\\; y = 2x + (n+20),\\dots\n\\]\n\nEach added line is sunny, distinct from all previous ones, and leaves the covering property unchanged.  \nAfter adding enough such lines we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nIf the total number of lines is still smaller than \\(n\\), we finally add any non‑sunny lines that miss \\(S_n\\) (e.g. \\(x=n+1,\\;y=n+1,\\;x+y=n+2\\)) until the family contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Case 2 – \\(k<m\\).*  \nBegin with the covering \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) and **remove** \\(m-k\\) of its sunny lines; the points that were covered only by the removed lines are still uncovered.  \nCover those remaining points by adding new sunny lines that avoid all points already covered (again possible because a line can be chosen to pass through any single uncovered point and to miss all others).  \nThus we obtain a family that uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(S_n\\).  \nIf the total number of lines is below \\(n\\), we supplement with arbitrary non‑sunny lines that miss \\(S_n\\) until we have \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\nIn both cases we have produced a set of **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines**, of which **exactly \\(k\\) are sunny**, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nInequality (1) shows that no configuration with more than \\(n\\) sunny lines can exist, because there are only \\(n\\) lines altogether.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
