  Three non‑sunny lines are unavoidable for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nConsider the three subsets of \\(T_{n}\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nR&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nD&=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of them consists of \\(n\\) points.  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\), then it can contain at most one point of each of the three sets\n(because two points of the same set would force the line to have a forbidden\nslope).  Consequently covering any of the three sets needs at least one line\nparallel respectively to the \\(y\\)-axis, to the \\(x\\)-axis and to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Hence any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain the three\nnon‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that remain uncovered after (5)\n\nRemoving the points of \\(C\\cup R\\cup D\\) from \\(T_{n}\\) leaves  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\mid 2\\le a,b,\\;a+b\\le n\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) is the set of integer points of the triangle with vertices\n\\((2,2),(2,n-2),(n-2,2)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:x+y=c . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{c}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is non‑sunny) and contains exactly  \n\n\\[\np(c)=c-3\\qquad (4\\le c\\le n)\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  The family \\(\\{D_{c}\\mid 4\\le c\\le n\\}\\) consists of\n\\(n-3\\) lines and together they cover all of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  How many of the diagonals can be omitted?\n\nA sunny line can intersect a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\