nas\n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Points that remain uncovered after (6)\n\nRemoving the points of \\(C\\cup R\\cup D\\) from \\(T_{n}\\) leaves  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\mid 2\\le a,b,\\;a+b\\le n\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) is the set of integer points of the triangle with vertices\n\\((2,2),(2,n-2),(n-2,2)\\); its cardinality is \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:x+y=c . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{c}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is non‑sunny) and contains exactly  \n\n\\[\np(c)=c-3\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  The family \\(\\{D_{c}\\mid 4\\le c\\le n\\}\\) consists of\n\\(n-3\\) lines and together they cover all of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. How many of the diagonals can be omitted?\n\nA sunny line can intersect a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point; otherwise\ntwo points with the same sum would lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently,\nto delete a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) we must cover its \\(p(c)=c-3\\) points by distinct\nsunny lines.  This is possible iff  \n\n\\[\nc-3\\le k, \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines we are allowed to use.\n\nThus with \\(k\\) sunny lines we may discard precisely those diagonals whose\nsums satisfy \\(c\\le k+3\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) such diagonals\n(\\(c=4,5,\\dots ,k+3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) in the indicated range.\n\n* Keep the three forced non‑sunny lines (6).  \n* Keep the diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) for \\(c=k+4,\\dots ,n\\); there are\n  \\(n-3-k\\) of them.  \n* Introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\):\n\n  \\[\n  S_{t}: y = x + t\\qquad (t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  For a discarded diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\le k+3\\) we have \\(c-3\\le k\\).\n  Write its points as \\((2+j,\\;c-2-j)\\) for \\(j=0,\\dots ,c-4\\) and assign the\n  point with index \\(j\\) to the sunny line \\(S_{j}\\).  Because each sunny line\n  receives at most one point from any diagonal, condition (9) is respected,\n  and every point of the discarded diagonals is covered.\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are therefore covered either by a kept diagonal\nor by one of the sunny lines (10).  Together with the three forced lines (6)\nthe total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n3\\;+\\;(n-3-k)\\;+\\;k = n .\n\\]\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); add a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\), e.g.\n  \\(y=x\\).  \n* \\(k=2\\): keep only \\(x=1\\); add two sunny lines, e.g. \\(y=x\\) and\n  \\(y=x+1\\), which together cover \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): take three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the\n  three points, with slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThus every \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Necessity of the bound\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the three forced lines (6) are unavoidable, so at most\n\\(n-3\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny.  The construction of §5 shows that\nevery integer \\(k\\) not exceeding this bound can indeed be realised.  Hence the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the answer.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
