is symmetric after swapping \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) and replacing the slope by its reciprocal).  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a sunny line among the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_k\\) is contained in at least one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines defined in (1)–(2).  \n   The lines are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.  \n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   Starting from the original integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we choose non‑sunny vertical/horizontal lines so that the uncovered part is a set \\(S_k\\) with the same parameter \\(k\\) as the desired number of sunny lines.  \n   By the constructions of Sections 2–4 we can cover this remaining set with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Consequently for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\ge 3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the case “all lines are sunny”\n\nFix non‑negative integers \\(m,p\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm+p=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);  \nevery point with \\(b\\le p\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nHence the points that are **not** covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na>m,\\; b>p,\\; a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m\\) and \\(b'=b-p\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge1,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n+1-(m+p)=k+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(S_k\\) (see (3) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)).  \nConsequently, to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines it suffices to exhibit, for the given \\(k\\), a family of **\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(S_k\\)**.  \nThe total number of lines will then be \\(|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+k = m+p+k = n\\), as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Covering \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe treat the three possibilities for \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the empty family covers the empty set \\(S_0\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y=2x-1\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Hence \\(S_1\\) is covered by one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=2\\) (impossibility)\n\n\\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points has slope  \n\n* \\(0\\) (the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)),  \n* \\(\\infty\\) (the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)), or  \n* \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \nConsequently at least three sunny lines would be necessary, contradicting the requirement of exactly two.  \nHence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(k\\) lines.\n\n*For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k-2\\)*\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x - (i-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Two additional lines*\n\\[\nL_{k-1}:\\; y = (k-1)x - (k-2),\\qquad\nL_{k}  :\\; y = \\frac{1}{k-1}x + \\frac{k-2}{k-1}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\nWe prove that every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case A: \\(a\\le b\\).**  \nWrite \\(b=a+t\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(2a+t\\le k+1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\na\\le \\frac{k+1-t}{2}\\le k-1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i=t+1\\).  \nSince \\(t\\le b-1\\le k-a\\le k-1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\).  \n\nSubstituting \\(i\\) into (9) gives the line  \n\n\\[\ny = (t+1)x - t .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating (12) at \\((a,b)=(a,a+t)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\na+t = (t+1)a - t \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a+t = a t + a - t \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; t = t,\n\\]\n\nwhich is true. Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_{t+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case B: \\(a>b\\).**  \nSwap the coordinates and apply the argument of Case A to the point \\((b,a)\\).  \nWe obtain that \\((b,a)\\) lies on a line \\(L_{t+1}\\) with \\(t=a-b\\ge0\\).  \nEquation (12) with this \\(t\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1}{t+1}y + \\frac{t}{t+1},\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the line \\(L_{k}\\) when \\(t=k-2\\) or one of the lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\le k-2\\) otherwise (the slopes \\(\\frac{1}{t+1}\\) are all distinct and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies the equation of a sunny line belonging to the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered.  \nSince the argument works for an arbitrary element of \\(S_k\\), the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the original problem\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\), choose non‑negative integers \\(m,p\\) with \\(m+p=n-k\\).  \nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nThe uncovered points form a set \\(S_k\\).  \n\n* If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we use the constructions of §2.1.  \n* If \\(k=2\\) the reduction shows that the uncovered set would be \\(S_2\\), which cannot be covered by two sunny lines (proved in §2.2); consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible for the original problem.  \n* If \\(k\\ge3\\) we employ the family of §2.3 to cover \\(S_k\\).  \n\nAltogether we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, **iff**  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\ge3\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non‑negative integers }k\\text{ with }k\\neq2\\text{ are attainable; i.e. }k=0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
