il\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\n   s=k-r=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Define two families of lines.\n\n   *Family \\(A\\) (slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,r\\))*  \n\n   \\[\n   A_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,r .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Family \\(B\\) (reciprocal slopes \\(1/2,1/3,\\dots ,1/(s+1)\\))*  \n\n   \\[\n   B_j:\\; x = (j+1)\\,y-j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,s .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All slopes occurring in (1) and (2) belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); consequently every line listed is sunny.  \n   The \\(r+s=k\\) lines are pairwise distinct because the slopes in (1) are positive integers \\(\\ge 1\\) whereas the slopes in (2) are the reciprocals of integers \\(\\ge 2\\).\n\n   **Coverage of \\(S_k\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n   *Case I: \\(b\\ge a\\).*  \n   Put \\(d=b-a\\;(d\\ge 0)\\).  \n   Since \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(d\\le k-1\\).  \n   If \\(d+1\\le r\\) we use line \\(A_{d+1}\\).  \n   Substituting \\((a,b)=(a,a+d)\\) into (1) with \\(i=d+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (d+1)a-d \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a+d = (d+1)a-d,\n   \\]\n\n   which holds identically; hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(A_{d+1}\\).\n\n   If \\(d+1>r\\) then \\(d\\ge r\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a \\le k+1-b = k+1-(a+d) = (k-d)-a+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   From \\(d\\ge r=\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) it follows that \\(k-d\\le \\lfloor k/2\\rfloor = s\\).  \n   Consequently \\(a\\le s+1\\).  \n   Set \\(j = a-1\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le s\\)).  \n   The line \\(B_j\\) has equation \\(x = (j+1)y-j\\).  \n   Substituting \\((a,b)=(j+1,b)\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a = (j+1)b-j \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; j+1 = (j+1)b-j,\n   \\]\n\n   which simplifies to \\(b = a+d\\) and is true by definition of \\(d\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(B_j\\).\n\n   *Case II: \\(a>b\\).*  \n   Swap the coordinates and apply the argument of Case I to the point \\((b,a)\\).  \n   If \\((b,a)\\) is covered by a line of family \\(A\\) (resp. \\(B\\)), then the same geometric line also contains \\((a,b)\\) because the equations in (1) and (2) are symmetric under the exchange of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) together with replacing the slope by its reciprocal.  \n   Therefore \\((a,b)\\) is also covered.\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(k\\) lines listed in (1)–(2).  \n   Thus the family \\(\\{A_1,\\dots ,A_r,B_1,\\dots ,B_s\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   For a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) :\n\n   * Choose non‑negative integers \\(m,p\\) with \\(m+p=n-k\\).  \n   * Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and the \\(p\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   * The uncovered points form the set \\(S_k\\).  \n   * If \\(k=0,1\\) use the constructions of §2.  \n   * If \\(k=2\\) the reduction shows that the uncovered set would be \\(S_2\\), which cannot be covered by two sunny lines (proved in §3); hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n   * If \\(k\\ge 3\\) use the family of §4, which consists of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(S_k\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\ge 3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the case “all lines are sunny”\n\nFix integers \\(m,p\\ge 0\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm+p=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);  \nevery point with \\(b\\le p\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nHence the points **not** covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na>m,\\;b>p,\\;a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m,\\;b'=b-p\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge 1,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n+1-(m+p)=k+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(S_k\\) (definition (3) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)).  \nConsequently, to obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones it suffices to exhibit, for the given \\(k\\), a family of **exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_k\\)**.  \nAdding the \\(m+p=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines from (5) then yields the required configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Covering \\(S_k\\) for the small values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The empty family covers the empty set \\(S_0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y=2x-1\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so at least three sunny lines would be necessary.  \n  Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\ns=k-r=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDefine two families of lines.\n\n*Family \\(A\\)* (slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,r\\))  \n\n\\[\nA_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,r .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Family \\(B\\)* (reciprocal slopes \\(1/2,1/3,\\dots ,1/(s+1)\\))  \n\n\\[\nB_j:\\; x = (j+1)\\,y-j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,s .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes occurring in (10) and (11) belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); consequently every line listed is sunny.  \nThe \\(r+s=k\\) lines are pairwise distinct because the slopes in (10) are positive integers \\(\\ge 1\\) whereas the slopes in (11) are the reciprocals of integers \\(\\ge 2\\); no equality can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Coverage proof\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); thus  \n\n\\[\na,b\\ge 1,\\qquad a+b\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case I: \\(d\\ge 0\\) (i.e. \\(b\\ge a\\)).**  \n\n*Subcase I‑1:* If \\(d+1\\le r\\) we use the line \\(A_{d+1}\\).  \nSubstituting \\((a,b)=(a,a+d)\\) into (10) with \\(i=d+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = (d+1)a-d \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a+d = (d+1)a-d,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity; hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(A_{d+1}\\).\n\n*Subcase I‑2:* If \\(d+1> r\\) then \\(d\\ge r\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na \\le k+1-b = k+1-(a+d) = (k-d)-a+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(d\\ge r=\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) we have \\(k-d\\le \\lfloor k/2\\rfloor = s\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\na\\le s+1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(j=a-1\\); then \\(1\\le j\\le s\\).  \nThe line \\(B_j\\) has equation \\(x=(j+1)y-j\\).  \nPlugging \\((a,b)=(j+1,b)\\) into this equation yields  \n\n\\[\na = (j+1)b-j \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; j+1 = (j+1)b-j,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(b=a+d\\) and is true by definition of \\(d\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(B_j\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case II: \\(d<0\\) (i.e. \\(a>b\\)).**  \n\nSwap the coordinates and consider the point \\((b,a)\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), the swapped point also belongs to \\(S_k\\).  \nApplying the argument of Case I to \\((b,a)\\) shows that \\((b,a)\\) lies on some line of family \\(A\\) or \\(B\\).  \nBoth families are symmetric under the exchange of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) together with replacing the slope by its reciprocal; therefore the same geometric line also contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n---\n\nAll possibilities have been exhausted; consequently **every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on at least one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (10)–(11).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the original problem\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\):\n\n* Choose any non‑negative integers \\(m,p\\) with \\(m+p=n-k\\).  \n* Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and the \\(p\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n* The uncovered points are exactly \\(S_k\\).  \n\n  *If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\)*, cover \\(S_k\\) with the constructions of §2.  \n  *If \\(k=2\\)*, the uncovered set would be \\(S_2\\), which cannot be covered by two sunny lines (proved in §2); therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n  *If \\(k\\ge 3\\)*, cover \\(S_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines described in §3.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+k = m+p+k = n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\ge 3\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
