f \\(n\\) distinct lines of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n* If \\(k=2\\), the reduction forces us to cover the set \\(S_2\\) with two sunny lines, which is impossible; therefore no such family exists.\n* If \\(k=3\\), the explicit three sunny lines of §2 together with the appropriate number of vertical/horizontal lines give a valid family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly three sunny ones.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor the given problem.  (The existence of configurations for larger values of \\(k\\) remains an open question within the scope of this answer.)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
