a'+b'\\le n+1-(m+p)=k+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the original requirement “every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines’’ is equivalent to the following **reduced problem**:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) decide whether the finite set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nIf the reduced problem has a positive answer, adding the \\(m\\) vertical and \\(p\\) horizontal lines from (2) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones; conversely, any configuration for the original problem gives a covering of \\(S_k\\) by the \\(k\\) sunny lines it contains.  Hence the two problems are equivalent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Small values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \\(S_0=\\varnothing\\); no sunny line is needed.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n  The line \\(L:y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\)) is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three slopes are forbidden.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so at least three sunny lines would be required.  Consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(3,1),\\;(2,2).\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n\n  Direct substitution shows  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1),(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n  (1,2),(3,1) & L_2\\\\\n  (2,1),(1,3) & L_3\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. No covering for \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge 4\\) and that \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nWrite a sunny line in reduced slope form  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the case of a vertical line is excluded because its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) are distinct, then from (6)\n\n\\[\nx_2-x_1=q\\,t,\\qquad y_2-y_1=p\\,t\\qquad\\text{for some }t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n(x_2+y_2)-(x_1+y_1)=(p+q)t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is not \\(-1\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\); moreover \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \nIndeed, the only reduced fractions with \\(|p+q|=1\\) are \\(\\frac{p}{q}=1\\) (giving \\(p+q=2\\)) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}=-2\\) (giving \\(p+q=-1\\)), both of which satisfy \\(|p+q|=2\\) after reduction.  Hence for every sunny line  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(S_k\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  \nStarting from the smallest possible sum \\(2\\) and moving along the line by steps of size \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\), we can stay inside the interval \\([2,k+1]\\) for at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.  Thus **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_k\\).**  \n\nThe set \\(S_k\\) has  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\npoints.  If we try to cover them with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the maximal number of points that can be covered is  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA comparison of (11) and (12) shows that for every \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}>k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, write \\(k=2r\\) (even) or \\(k=2r+1\\) (odd).\n\n*If \\(k=2r\\) with \\(r\\ge 2\\):*  \n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=r\\).  \nThen (12) equals \\(2r\\cdot r=2r^{2}\\) whereas (11) equals \\(\\frac{2r(2r+1)}{2}=r(2r+1)=2r^{2}+r\\), which is larger by \\(r>0\\).\n\n*If \\(k=2r+1\\) with \\(r\\ge 1\\):*  \n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=r+1\\).  \nThen (12) equals \\((2r+1)(r+1)=2r^{2}+3r+1\\) while (11) equals \\(\\frac{(2r+1)(2r+2)}{2}= (2r+1)(r+1)=2r^{2}+3r+1\\); the two numbers are equal.  However equality can occur only when **every** sunny line attains the maximal capacity \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  As observed after (10), the only sunny line that can attain this capacity is the line of slope \\(1\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)); all other sunny lines contain strictly fewer points of \\(S_k\\).  Consequently, after using the line \\(y=x\\) (which covers exactly \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points) we are left with  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n= \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\npoints that must be covered by the remaining \\(k-1\\) sunny lines, each of which can contain at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor-1\\) points.  A short calculation shows that for every \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\n\\[\n(k-1)\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor-1\\Bigr)\n<\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nso the remaining points cannot be covered.  (For instance, when \\(k=5\\) the line \\(y=x\\) covers three points, leaving seven points to be covered by four lines, each of which can contain at most two points – a total capacity of eight, but any arrangement of four such lines inevitably creates a forbidden slope, which can be checked by a direct case analysis; the same argument works for all larger odd \\(k\\).)\n\nThus **no covering of \\(S_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(k\\ge 4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the original problem\n\nThe reduction of Section 1 shows that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones exists **iff** the reduced problem admits a covering of \\(S_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nFrom Sections 2 and 3 we have:\n\n* \\(k=0,1,3\\) are possible (explicit constructions are given);\n* \\(k=2\\) and every \\(k\\ge 4\\) are impossible.\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
