sible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(t=n-k\\) diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   D_{c}: x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   They cover every point of \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) does not exceed \\(t+1\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining points are those with \\(a+b\\ge t+2\\).  \n   Write \\(n=2r\\) (even) or \\(n=2r+1\\) (odd).  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-r\\le d\\le r\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_{d}: x-y=d .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   By (1) each \\(L_{d}\\) contains exactly \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(P_{n}\\); moreover the collection \\(\\{L_{d}\\mid -r\\le d\\le r\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (they are all of slope \\(1\\), which is allowed).\n\n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we keep the first \\(k\\) of the lines \\(L_{d}\\) (for instance those with the smallest \\(d\\)).  \n   Because the diagonal lines (4) already cover all points with sum \\(\\le t+1\\), the points that are not yet covered lie on the chosen \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{d}\\); each of those points belongs to exactly one of the kept \\(L_{d}\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(P_{n}\\) is covered, the total number of lines is \\(t+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   This construction works for every \\(k\\) satisfying (3).  \n\n6. **Impossibility for the excluded case.**  \n   When \\(n\\) is even and \\(k=n\\), all lines are sunny.  \n   By (1) each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points, so the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n/2=n^{2}/2\\) points, which is strictly smaller than \\(|P_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n   Hence the case \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\) cannot occur.\n\nCombining the construction with the impossibility argument yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal size of a sunny line\n\nFor a line of slope \\(1\\) we have the equation \\(x-y=d\\) with an integer\n\\(d\\).  \nThe conditions \\(a-b=d,\\;a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\max (1,1+d)\\le a\\le \\min \\bigl(n,\\;n+1-d\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence the number of lattice points of \\(P_{n}\\) on the line \\(x-y=d\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in P_{n}\\mid a-b=d\\}= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to the three forbidden directions,\ntherefore any sunny line can contain at most the number in (7).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains }\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\text{ points of }P_{n}.}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) required lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and\n\\(t=n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical or diagonal).  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P_{n}\\)\n(e.g. the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)).\nUsing the maximal capacities we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor + t\\;n \\;\\ge\\; |P_{n}|\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-k\\) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible; for odd \\(n\\) (10)\nplaces no restriction on \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (10) and put \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(t\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}: \\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_{c}\\) belongs to the forbidden family, hence it is non\u2011sunny.\nAll points of \\(P_{n}\\) with \\(a+b\\le t+1\\) lie on one of the lines\n\\(D_{c}\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny lines  \n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nn=\n\\begin{cases}\n2r,   & n\\text{ even},\\\\\n2r+1, & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(d\\) with \\(-r\\le d\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}: \\;x-y=d .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_{d}\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\nBy (7) each \\(L_{d}\\) contains exactly \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(P_{n}\\), and the collection \\(\\{L_{d}\\mid -r\\le d\\le r\\}\\)\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nFrom these \\(n\\) lines we keep the first \\(k\\) of them (for instance the\nlines with the smallest values of \\(d\\)); denote the kept set by\n\\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{d_{1}},\\dots ,L_{d_{k}}\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Verification of the covering  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in P_{n}\\) falls into one of two categories.\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le t+1\\)*, it lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\) and is\ncovered.\n\n*If \\(a+b\\ge t+2\\)*, then \\(a-b\\) is an integer between \\(-r\\) and\n\\(r\\).  Moreover, for each fixed value of \\(a-b\\) there is exactly one\npoint of \\(P_{n}\\) with a given sum \\(c\\ge t+2\\).  Consequently the\nremaining points are precisely the points of the lines \\(L_{d}\\) with\n\\(d\\) ranging over the set \\(\\{-r,\\dots ,r\\}\\) that have not been\ndiscarded.  By keeping the first \\(k\\) of those lines we guarantee that\nevery point with \\(a+b\\ge t+2\\) belongs to one of the kept lines\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(P_{n}\\) is covered by one of the \\(t\\) diagonal\nlines (11) or one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (13).  The total number of\nlines is \\(t+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (10).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of the excluded case\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is even and that all \\(n\\) required lines are sunny.\nBy (8) each of them contains at most \\(n/2\\) points of \\(P_{n}\\); hence\nthe whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Because \\(|P_{n}|=n(n+1)/2 > n^{2}/2\\) for even \\(n\\), the\nset \\(P_{n}\\) cannot be covered, contradicting the hypothesis.  Therefore\n\\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the construction of Section\u202f3 with the impossibility proved in\nSection\u202f4 yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of\nsunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
