.*  \n   Let \\(t=n-k\\).  Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the first \\(t\\) rows.  All points whose sum \\(s\\le t+1\\) are therefore already covered.\n\n   *Step 2 – columns.*  \n   Write  \n\n   \\[\n   n=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   2r,   & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\\n   2r+1, & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\bigl\\{\\, -r,\\,-r+1,\\dots , -r+k-1 \\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(C\\) consists of \\(k\\) consecutive integers; all of them have the same parity as \\(n\\), which guarantees that each column in \\(C\\) actually occurs in the board (by (4)).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a point with sum \\(s\\ge t+2\\).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   s-2\\ge t=n-k\\ge k-1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the interval of admissible differences for this row,\n   \\([-(s-2),\\,s-2]\\) (step 2), contains the whole block of integers \\([-r,\\,-r+k-1]\\) that defines \\(C\\).  Consequently the difference \\(d=a-b\\) belongs to \\(C\\), and the point is covered by the sunny line \\(x-y=d\\).\n\n   Thus every point of the set \\(\\{(a,b):a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies either on one of the rows (5) or on one of the columns (7).  The corresponding geometric lines are:\n\n   * the \\(t=n-k\\) anti‑diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\in R\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(x-y=d\\) with \\(d\\in C\\).\n\n   All these lines are distinct, their total number is \\(t+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3).  \n\n7. **Impossibility of the excluded case.**  \n   When \\(n\\) is even and \\(k=n\\) all lines would be sunny.  By (1) each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points, so \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n/2=n^{2}/2\\) points.  Since \\(|\\{(a,b):a+b\\le n+1\\}|=n(n+1)/2>n^{2}/2\\), the required covering is impossible.  \n\nCombining the construction with the impossibility argument yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
