he points of \\(T_n\\) that are **not** covered by the vertical\nlines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, if we can exhibit, for every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\), a family of exactly\n\\(m\\) sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_m\\), then adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical\nlines (3) to that family for \\(m=k\\) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny members that covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny covering of the triangle \\(T_m\\)\n\nLet \\(m\\ge 1\\) be fixed.  For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le m+1\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;\\text{the line through }(j,1)\\text{ and }(1,m+2-j).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(m+2-j)}{j-1}= -1+\\frac{1}{j-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(j\\ge 2\\), this number is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never\n\\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_m\\).*  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).\nSet \\(j:=a+1\\); then \\(2\\le j\\le m+1\\).  The line \\(L_j\\) passes through the\npoints \\((j,1)=(a+1,1)\\) and \\((1,m+2-j)=(1,m+1-a)\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) we\nhave \\(b\\le m+1-a\\); hence the three points \\((a,b),\\;(a+1,1),\\;(1,m+1-a)\\) are\ncollinear.  Indeed, the slope of the segment joining \\((a+1,1)\\) and\n\\((1,m+1-a)\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(m+1-a)}{a+1-1}= -1+\\frac{1}{a},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the slope of \\(L_{a+1}\\) given by (5).  Therefore\n\\((a,b)\\in L_{a+1}\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to some line \\(L_j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le\nm+1\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m:=\\{L_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le m+1\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the whole set\n\\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(v_i\\) defined in (3).  They are non‑sunny.\n* Use the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) defined in (6) with \\(m=k\\).  It\n  consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers the remaining set\n  \\(T_k\\) (see (4)).\n\nThe union of these two families contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nwhich are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  Hence for the given\n\\(n\\) the required configuration exists for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Verification for the minimal case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor completeness we list explicit configurations for \\(n=3\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (sunny / non‑sunny) | Remarks |\n|------|---------------------------|---------|\n| 0 | \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) (all non‑sunny) | Covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)). | All six points are covered. |\n| 2 | \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny), sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), sunny line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) (slope \\(1\\)). | All six points are covered. |\n| 3 | Sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)); sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)); sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)). | All six points are covered. |\n\nThese examples agree with the general construction described above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. No