sider the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and cover every point of the set  \n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\nwhose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe set of points that remain uncovered is  \n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\nDefine the translation \\(\\tau:(x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have\n\\[\n\\tau(a,b)=(a-(n-k),b),\\qquad\n(a-(n-k))+b\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n\\]\nand both coordinates are positive.  \nThus \\(\\tau\\) maps \\(U_{n,k}\\) bijectively onto  \n\\[\nT_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_k\\), then the translated family\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k'=\\{\\ell+(n-k,0)\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal L_k\\}\n\\]\nconsists of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U_{n,k}\\).  \nAdding the vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_{n-k}\\) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the original covering requirement and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, given any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, an affine change of coordinates (preserving the three forbidden directions) can be performed so that the non\u2011sunny lines become vertical.  After this transformation the uncovered points are again a translate of \\(T_k\\), showing that the existence of a sunny\u2011line cover of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) lines is necessary.\n\nHence the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a required configuration of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** the triangular lattice set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Partial solution of the aux