            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*Notation.*  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{-1}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) are parallel to \\(\\mathbf{e}_{-1}\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=x\n\\]\n\n(the slope \\(1\\) line) and, for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(v_i\\) are vertical, therefore non‑sunny; \\(\\ell\\) is sunny because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 1\\); the only possible point is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=n\\) after translating the whole picture by the vector \\((-(n-1),0)\\); equivalently, the line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) after the same translation, and a translation does not change the property of being sunny.  Hence \\((n,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one of them (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nWe shall derive a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\nAn affine map of the plane of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)\\longmapsto (x,\\;y+ \\alpha x)\n\\qquad(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R)\n\\]\n\npreserves the three forbidden directions \\(\\mathbf{e}_x,\\mathbf{e}_y,\\mathbf{e}_{-1}\\); indeed it sends each of the three direction vectors to a non‑zero scalar multiple of itself.  Applying a suitable shear of this type we can send any non‑sunny line that is not already vertical to a vertical line, while leaving the two sunny lines sunny (their slopes are changed but remain different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Consequently we may assume, without loss of generality, that all \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) are vertical:\n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and pairwise parallel, hence non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  The uncovered set  \n\nThe vertical lines \\(v_i\\) cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-2\\).  \nTherefore the set of points that remain uncovered is\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(n-2)\\) and keeping \\(b\\) unchanged gives a bijection  \n\n\\[\nU\\longrightarrow T_2:=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\},\n\\qquad (a,b)\\mapsto (a',b).\n\\]\n\nThus, after the translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-2),y)\\), the uncovered points are exactly the three points of \\(T_2\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Two sunny lines cannot cover \\(T_2\\)\n\nConsider any line that contains two distinct points of \\(T_2\\).\n\n* The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) is vertical.  \n* The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is horizontal.  \n* The line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nAll three possibilities are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, i.e. they are **non‑sunny**.  Consequently no line that is sunny can contain two points of \\(T_2\\); a sunny line can meet \\(T_2\\) in at most one point.  Since \\(|T_2|=3\\), covering \\(T_2\\) would require at least three sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Reduction to a triangular covering problem\n\nFor a general integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we first place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation  \n\n\\[\n\\tau:(x,y)\\longmapsto (x-(n-k),\\,y)\n\\]\n\nmaps \\(U_{n,k}\\) bijectively onto  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the original problem is equivalent to the following auxiliary problem.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> There exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof of “if’’.*  \nAssume that \\(T_k\\) is covered by sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \nTranslate each \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the translated lines \\(\\ell_i+(n-k,0)\\) are still sunny and together cover \\(U_{n,k}\\).  Adding the vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_{n-k}\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof of “only‑if’’.*  \nConversely, let a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines be given.  \nApply the shear \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x,\\;y+\\alpha x)\\) with a suitable \\(\\alpha\\) so that all non‑sunny lines become vertical (this is possible because each non‑sunny direction can be sent to the vertical direction by an appropriate shear, while the three forbidden directions are preserved).  After this affine change the uncovered points are precisely a translate of \\(T_k\\), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines of the transformed configuration give a sunny‑line cover of \\(T_k\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Consequences for small values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The set \\(T_0\\) is empty, so the covering condition is vacuous.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible (the construction of § 1).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The set \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) is covered by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); translating this line back gives the construction of § 2.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is always possible.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  The set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines, because any line containing two of these points is non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  By Lemma 1 this shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  The problem reduces to the existence of a sunny‑line cover of the triangular set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) lines.  No general construction (or impossibility proof) is known at present; the question remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\nand the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Whether any of the integers \\(k\\ge 3\\) can occur is an open problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
