r of \\(T_k\\) back and adding the vertical lines \\(v_i\\); the “only‑if’’ direction follows from the shear argument used in the proof of the impossibility of \\(k=2\\).\n\n6. **Partial resolution of the auxiliary problem.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\): \\(T_0\\) is empty, so the condition is vacuous.  \n   * \\(k=1\\): \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) is covered by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\).  \n   * \\(k=2\\): \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines, because any line containing two of these points is non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   * The cases \\(k\\ge3\\) remain open: no general construction of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\) is known, nor is a proof of impossibility.  \n\nConsequently the admissible numbers of sunny lines are known to be \\(0\\) and \\(1\\); the value \\(2\\) is impossible; the status of all integers \\(k\\ge3\\) is still undetermined.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*Notation.*  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{-1}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) are parallel to \\(\\mathbf{e}_{-1}\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines; all are non‑sunny.  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=2(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny and distinct from all the vertical lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=2(n-n)+1\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one of them (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\nAn affine map of the form  \n\n\\[\nT_\\alpha:(x,y)\\longmapsto (x,\\;y+\\alpha x)\\qquad(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R)\n\\]\n\npreserves the three forbidden directions \\(\\mathbf{e}_x,\\mathbf{e}_y,\\mathbf{e}_{-1}\\); indeed each of the three direction vectors is an eigenvector of the linear part of \\(T_\\alpha\\).  By choosing a suitable \\(\\alpha\\) we can send any non‑sunny line (which is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions) to a vertical line, while a sunny line remains sunny because its slope is changed but never becomes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Applying an appropriate shear we may therefore assume that all \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) are vertical:\n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and pairwise parallel, hence non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  The uncovered set  \n\nThe vertical lines \\(v_i\\) cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-2\\).  \nConsequently the set of points that remain uncovered is\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(n-2)\\) and keeping \\(b\\) unchanged gives a bijection  \n\n\\[\nU\\longrightarrow T_2:=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\},\n\\qquad (a,b)\\mapsto (a',b).\n\\]\n\nThus, after the translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-2),y)\\), the uncovered points are exactly the three points of \\(T_2\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Two sunny lines cannot cover \\(T_2\\)\n\nConsider any line that contains two distinct points of \\(T_2\\).\n\n* The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) is vertical.  \n* The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is horizontal.  \n* The line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nAll three possibilities are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, i.e. they are **non‑sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can meet \\(T_2\\) in at most one point.  Since \\(|T_2|=3\\), covering \\(T_2\\) would require at least three sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Reduction to a triangular covering problem\n\nFor a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and cover all points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x\\le n-k\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation  \n\n\\[\n\\tau:(x,y)\\longmapsto (x-(n-k),\\,y)\n\\]\n\nmaps \\(U_{n,k}\\) bijectively onto  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the original problem is equivalent to the following auxiliary problem.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> There exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a cover of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) is given, translate each \\(\\ell_i\\) back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the translated lines are still sunny and together cover \\(U_{n,k}\\).  Adding the vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_{n-k}\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, start from a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Apply a shear \\(T_\\alpha\\) as in § 3.1 so that all non‑sunny lines become vertical.  After this affine change the uncovered points are precisely a translate of \\(T_k\\), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines of the transformed configuration give a sunny‑line cover of \\(T_k\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Consequences for small values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The set \\(T_0\\) is empty, so the covering condition is vacuous.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible (the construction of § 1).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The set \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) is covered by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); translating back gives the construction of § 2.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is always possible.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  The set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines, because any line containing two of these points is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), all of which are forbidden.  By Lemma 1 this shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  The auxiliary problem asks for a cover of the triangular lattice \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No general construction (or impossibility proof) is known at present; consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) for \\(k\\ge3\\) remain undetermined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are known to include \\(0\\) and \\(1\\); the value \\(2\\) is impossible.  The existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge3\\) sunny lines has not been settled; the original problem therefore remains open beyond the cases treated above."
