ith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f6.1 \u2013 Choice of the sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (i+1)\\,x - i(i+1) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(i+1\\); because \\(i+1\\ge 2\\) we have\n   \\(\\,i+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover the lines are pairwise distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Step\u202f6.2 \u2013 Verification that \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers \\(T_k\\).*  \n   Take an arbitrary point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+y\\le k+1\\).  \n   Set \\(i:=x\\) (note that \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) because \\(x\\le x+y\\le k+1\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(i=x\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = (x+1)\\,x - x(x+1)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is zero, which is not the actual \\(y\\).  Hence the point \\((x,y)\\) does **not** lie on \\(\\ell_x\\) in general.  We therefore modify the construction.\n\n   *Corrected construction.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x + (k+2-i) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope is \\(i-1\\).  For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden; therefore we start the index at \\(i=2\\).  To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace the horizontal line \\(\\ell_1\\) by a sunny line of slope \\(2\\) that passes through the same point; the replacement does not affect the covering property (see below).  Consequently we will actually use the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\bigl\\{\\;y = (i-1)\\,x + (k+2-i)\\mid i=2,3,\\dots ,k\\;\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{\\,y = 2x + (k-2)\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) have slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\) or \\(2\\); none of these slopes equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny, and the total number of lines is \\(k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f6.3 \u2013 Covering proof.*