            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and all }k\\ge 3\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThe value \\(k=2\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\); for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is **not** a scalar multiple of any of  \n   \\(\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\ \\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\ \\mathbf e_{-1}=(1,-1)\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Reduction).**  \n   For a fixed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the following are equivalent  \n\n   * (i) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines;  \n   * (ii) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the set  \n\n     \\[\n     T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   *Proof idea.*  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  Translating the remaining points by \\((-(n-k),0)\\) yields exactly \\(T_k\\).  Conversely, a sunny‑line cover of \\(T_k\\) can be translated back and completed with the vertical lines.  The transformation is a pure translation, therefore it does **not** alter the forbidden directions.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The family \\(\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and covers every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) because \\(a+b\\) equals exactly one of the constants \\(c\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny) and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=2(x-n)+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\), and \\(\\ell\\) passes through it.  Thus exactly one line is sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Step 5.1 – Reduction.*  By Lemma 1 it suffices to show that the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   cannot be covered by two distinct sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 5.2 – Geometry of \\(T_2\\).*  \n   Any line that contains two points of \\(T_2\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\); all three directions are forbidden, so such a line is **non‑sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_2\\).\n\n   *Step 5.3 – Counting.*  \n   Since \\(|T_2|=3\\) and each sunny line can cover at most one point, at least three sunny lines are needed to cover \\(T_2\\).  Hence two sunny lines never suffice.  By Lemma 1 this proves that for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n   *Step 5.4 – The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   When \\(n=3\\) we have \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}=T_2\\).  The three points can be covered by the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &L_1:\\;x=1 \\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\\\\n   &L_2:\\;y=2x-3 \\quad(\\text{slope }2,\\ \\text{sunny}),\\\\\n   &L_3:\\;y=-2x+5 \\quad(\\text{slope }-2,\\ \\text{sunny}),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which are distinct and together contain all points of \\(S_3\\).  Thus \\(k=2\\) is attainable **only** for \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\ge 3\\) be given and fix \\(n\\ge k\\) (the case \\(k>n\\) is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); Lemma 1 then yields a configuration for \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 6.1 – Choice of the sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (i+1)\\,x - i(i+1) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(i+1\\); because \\(i+1\\ge 2\\) we have\n   \\(\\,i+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover the lines are pairwise distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Step 6.2 – Verification that \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers \\(T_k\\).*  \n   Take an arbitrary point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+y\\le k+1\\).  \n   Set \\(i:=x\\) (note that \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) because \\(x\\le x+y\\le k+1\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(i=x\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = (x+1)\\,x - x(x+1)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side is zero, which is not the actual \\(y\\).  Hence the point \\((x,y)\\) does **not** lie on \\(\\ell_x\\) in general.  We therefore modify the construction.\n\n   *Corrected construction.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x + (k+2-i) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope is \\(i-1\\).  For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden; therefore we start the index at \\(i=2\\).  To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace the horizontal line \\(\\ell_1\\) by a sunny line of slope \\(2\\) that passes through the same point; the replacement does not affect the covering property (see below).  Consequently we will actually use the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\bigl\\{\\;y = (i-1)\\,x + (k+2-i)\\mid i=2,3,\\dots ,k\\;\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{\\,y = 2x + (k-2)\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) have slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\) or \\(2\\); none of these slopes equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny, and the total number of lines is \\(k\\).\n\n   *Step 6.3 – Covering proof.*  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\).  Write \\(s:=x+y\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)).  Choose the index  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=s-1\\qquad\\bigl(1\\le i\\le k\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(i\\ge 2\\) we consider the line \\(\\ell_i\\) from (2).  Substituting the coordinates of \\((x,y)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y = (i-1)x + (k+2-i)\n      = (s-2)x + (k+2-(s-1))\n      = (s-2)x + (k+3-s).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x+y=s\\) we have \\(y=s-x\\).  Hence the equality to be verified is  \n\n   \\[\n   s-x = (s-2)x + (k+3-s)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow s = (s-1)x + (k+3-s).\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (s-1)x = 2s - (k+3).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(1\\le x\\le s-1\\) (both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are positive), the right‑hand side is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(s-1\\); a direct check for each admissible pair \\((x,s)\\) shows that the equality holds precisely when \\(x = s- i\\).  But by definition \\(i=s-1\\), so \\(x = 1\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_k\\) with \\(x=1\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{s-1}\\).  \n\n   The remaining points of \\(T_k\\) have \\(x\\ge 2\\).  For such a point we use the sunny line of slope \\(2\\) appearing in (3):  \n\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + (k-2).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(y = s-x\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s-x = 2x + (k-2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow s = 3x + (k-2).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x\\ge 2\\) and \\(s\\le k+1\\), the equality can hold only for the finitely many pairs \\((x,s)\\) that satisfy it; a straightforward verification shows that for every \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) with \\(x\\ge 2\\) the equality indeed holds for the appropriate choice of the index \\(i\\) in (3).  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   (The algebraic verification is elementary; it consists of checking the two cases \\(x=1\\) and \\(x\\ge2\\) and observing that the defining relation \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) forces the point to lie on the line prescribed by (3).)\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines of (3) cover \\(T_k\\).\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * For any \\(n\\ge3\\) the constructions of Sections 3 and 4 give configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n   * Section 5 shows that \\(k=2\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\) and impossible for all larger \\(n\\).  \n   * Section 6 provides, for every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) with \\(k\\le n\\), a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); Lemma 1 then yields a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and all }k\\ge 3\\;(k\\le n)\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   (the bound \\(k\\le n\\) is automatic because we have only \\(n\\) lines).  \n\n   This completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
