s of \\(C_1\\) can be covered by sunny lines; at least one point of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \n\nA non‑sunny line that is not vertical is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\); both meet \\(C_1\\) in at most one point.  \nHence to cover the whole column \\(C_1\\) we would need **two** non‑sunny lines (one vertical and one horizontal/diagonal), which together with the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would give at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines – a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Horizontal lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-k-1\\) of them, they are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the line \\(H_b\\); hence all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k-1\\) are already covered.\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x + (n-k-1-t).\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny.  \nBecause the intercepts \\(n-k-1-t\\) are distinct, the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\nLet us verify that the union of the \\(k+1\\) lines \\(L_t\\) covers the remaining points\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFix a row index \\(b\\) with \\(n-k\\le b\\le n\\).  \nFor a given \\(t\\) the intersection of \\(L_t\\) with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(b-(n-k-1-t),\\,b\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge n-k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-(n-k-1-t)\\le n+1-b,\n\\]\n\nthe latter inequality being equivalent to \\(b\\le n\\).  \nThus the point \\((b-(n-k-1-t),b)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nConsequently **each** row \\(b\\) contains **exactly one** point of every line \\(L_t\\); the set of all such points for \\(t=0,\\dots ,k\\) is precisely the whole row segment \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}\\).  \n\nHence the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_k\\) together cover all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Counting the lines\n\nWe have used  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\\underbrace{(k+1)}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nAmong them exactly \\(k\\) are sunny (the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\); the line \\(L_k\\) may be replaced by any of the horizontal lines without changing the total number of sunny lines).  \n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe upper bound proved in §1 shows that no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly the integers  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
