S_n\\) then  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow a_0+b_0+t(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the case \\(p+q=0\\) would give slope \\(-1\\)), the\ninequality bounds the integer parameter \\(t\\) from both sides; consequently\nat most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent values of \\(t\\) are possible.  Hence a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequence for the case \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\).\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot M\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n=2m\\) is even, then \\(M=\\frac{n}{2}=m\\) and the left–hand side of (2)\nequals \\(nm=n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2\\), which is **strictly smaller**\nthan \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Hence (2) cannot hold; for even \\(n\\) it is\nimpossible to cover all points with only sunny lines.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{for even }n,\\;k=n\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, then \\(M=\\frac{n+1}{2}=m+1\\) and (2) becomes an\nequality:\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot M = (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) the bound is sharp; it remains to exhibit a concrete\nfamily of \\(n\\) sunny lines that attains it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for odd \\(n\\) (\\(k=n\\))\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; \\text{through the two points }P_i=(i,1)\\text{ and }Q_i=(1,2m+2-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2m+2-i-1}{1-i}= \\frac{2m+1-i}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\) (because \\(i\\neq 2m+2\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (because\n\\(i\\neq1\\)), and never \\(-1\\) (the equality \\(\\frac{2m+1-i}{i-1}=-1\\) would\ngive \\(i= m+1\\), but then the numerator and denominator have opposite signs,\nso the ratio is not \\(-1\\)).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct.\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains the whole lattice segment joining \\(P_i\\) and\n\\(Q_i\\); consequently it contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\le i,\\;b\\le 2m+2-i\\}\\,| = m+1 = M\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Therefore the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) reaches the bound (1).\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\), at least one of the\ninequalities \\(a\\le m+1\\) or \\(b\\le m+1\\) holds.\n\n* If \\(a\\le m+1\\) then \\(a\\le i\\) for \\(i=a\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\nsegment \\(P_aQ_a\\), hence on \\(L_a\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le m+1\\) then \\(b\\le 2m+2-i\\) for \\(i=2m+2-b\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{2m+2-b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\).  Hence for odd \\(n\\) the configuration with\n\\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny, and together they already contain every point of\n\\(S_n\\) (every point has an \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)).  \nNow add any \\(k\\) further distinct lines whose slopes are different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); these added lines are sunny.  The total number of lines is\n\\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the covering condition is still\nsatisfied.  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists,\nindependently of the parity of \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCollecting the results of Sections 2–4 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n0\\le k\\le n, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n0\\le k\\le n-1, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. ∎"
