n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, (L) would give  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot M\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For even \\(n\\) we have \\(M=\\frac{n}{2}\\), whence\n   \\(nM=\\frac{n^{2}}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\); the inequality cannot hold.\n   Hence for even \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(n\\) with \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\text{ the line through }P_i=(i,1)\\text{ and }Q_i=(1,2m+2-i).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{2m+1-i}{i-1}\\), which is never\n   \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct.\n\n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\); hence either\n   \\(a\\le m+1\\) or \\(b\\le m+1\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le m+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the segment \\(P_aQ_a\\), i.e. on\n   \\(L_a\\).  If \\(b\\le m+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2m+2-b}\\).\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(L_i\\), and\n   we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction for all other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are not sunny and already contain every point of \\(S_n\\)\n   (each point has an \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)).  \n   Add any \\(k\\) further distinct lines whose slopes are different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); these added lines are sunny.  The total number of\n   lines is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the covering\n   condition remains satisfied.\n\nCombining the impossibility for even \\(n\\) with the explicit constructions\nabove yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\b