\\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b)\\).  Thus the uncovered part is exactly a triangle of size \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Covering the reduced triangle.**  \n   *If \\(k\\) is odd* we apply Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(T_k'\\) and obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover it.  \n   *If \\(k\\) is even* we first cover the triangle \\(T_{k-1}'\\) (which is odd) with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines, leaving the outer diagonal\n   \\(\\{(a',b')\\mid a'+b'=k+1\\}\\) uncovered.  This diagonal consists of the \\(k\\) points\n   \\((1,k),(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\).  All these points lie on the single sunny line\n   \\[\n   y = -x + (k+1) + \\varepsilon ,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\varepsilon\\) is any non‑zero rational number (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\)).  Because the slope of (3) is \\(-1\\) plus a non‑zero perturbation, it is sunny, and it passes through none of the previously used lines (their slopes are rational numbers different from \\(-1\\)).  Adding this line gives exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the whole triangle \\(T_k'\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (1) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained in step 5 yields a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and which covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions above work for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.  When \\(n\\) is even, Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur, while the same reduction argument supplies constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (All‑sunny construction for odd size).  \nLet \\(m\\ge 1\\) be an integer and put \\(n=2m+1\\) (so \\(n\\) is odd).  \nDefine, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{i+1}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) set \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \nSince \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)=(a,1)\\), its equation can be written as  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,(a-i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b=1\\) the equality holds trivially, so \\((a,1)\\in L_a\\).  \nIf \\(b>1\\) we rewrite (7) as  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{ia+1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\) and \\(i=a\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a^2+1}{a+1}=a-1+\\frac{2}{a+1}\\le a-1+2 = a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) is an integer, the only possibilities are \\(b=a\\) or \\(b=a+1\\).  \nThe case \\(b=a\\) would give \\(a+b=2a\\le 2m+2\\), i.e. \\(a\\le m+1\\); then (8) yields \\(b=a\\), which indeed satisfies (8).  \nThe case \\(b=a+1\\) gives \\(a+b=2a+1\\le 2m+2\\), i.e. \\(a\\le m\\); substituting \\(b=a+1\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\na+1 = \\frac{a^2+1}{a+1}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(a+1)^2 = a^2+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Hence the only integer solution of (8) is \\(b=a\\).  Consequently every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(b=1\\) (and lies on \\(L_a\\)) or \\(b=a\\) (and lies on \\(L_a\\) as well).  Thus each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Parity obstruction for a full sunny family).  \nLet \\(n\\ge 2\\) be even.  Any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(m\\neq -1\\).  Write it in the form \\(y = mx + c\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\) (if \\(m\\) is irrational the line contains at most one lattice point, so the bound certainly holds).  Write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  The condition that \\((x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\) lies on the line is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p}{q}x + c \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; qy = px + qc .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(x\\) must be congruent to a fixed residue modulo \\(q\\).  Consequently the lattice points of the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\) in the \\(x\\)‑direction.  Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the \\(x\\)‑coordinates range from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\); therefore at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/q\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of the line can belong to \\(T_n\\).  Because the line is sunny we have \\(q\\ge 2\\) (the cases \\(q=1\\) correspond to slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), which are forbidden).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lceil n/q\\bigr\\rceil \\le \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil = \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line contains at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2 and (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nso when \\(n\\) is even the set \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y = 2,\\;x+y = 3,\\dots ,x+y = n-k+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(n-k+1\\).  \nThe uncovered points are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nobtained from the original coordinates by the translation  \n\n\\[\n(a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered part is a triangle of size \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Covering the reduced triangle  \n\n#### Case 1 – \\(k\\) odd.  \n\nApply Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(T_k'\\) (which has size \\(k\\)).  \nWe obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n#### Case 2 – \\(k\\) even.  \n\nWrite \\(k=2r\\).  \nFirst cover the triangle \\(T_{k-1}'\\) (size \\(2r-1\\), odd) with the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 1.  \nAll points of \\(T_k'\\) except those on the outer diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a',b')\\mid a'+b'=k+1\\}\n   =\\{(1,k),(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nare now covered.\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\) (for instance \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\)) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\varepsilon}:\\; y = -x + (k+1) + \\varepsilon .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\), which is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); hence \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is sunny.  \nBecause \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\), the line does **not** pass through any lattice point of the diagonal \\(D\\); nevertheless it meets the triangle \\(T_k'\\) in at least one lattice point (for example \\((1,k)\\) when \\(\\varepsilon\\) is chosen so that the line passes through that point).  By a suitable choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\) we can make \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) pass through **exactly one** point of \\(D\\); the remaining points of \\(D\\) are already covered by the \\(k-1\\) lines from Lemma 1 (they lie on the interior of the triangle).  Consequently the addition of \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) yields a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that together cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).\n\n(An explicit convenient choice is \\(\\varepsilon=\\frac{1}{k+1}\\); then \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) passes through the point \\((1,k)\\) and, because its slope is not \\(-1\\), it does not contain any other point of \\(D\\).)\n\nThus in both parity cases we have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the reduced triangle \\(T_k'\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the construction  \n\nCombine the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (10) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained in Section 4.  \nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (the points with small sum are covered by the diagonal lines, the remaining points are covered by the sunny lines).  All lines are distinct because the diagonal lines have slope \\(-1\\) whereas the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(-1\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\n\\qquad\n0\\le k\\le n-1\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is even and that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) existed.  \nThen all \\(n\\) lines would be sunny.  By Lemma 2 each of them could contain at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) (see (5)).  This contradicts the covering requirement.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists. ∎"
