hese cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum is at most \\(n-k+1\\).  \n   The uncovered points form the triangle\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   obtained from \\(T_n\\) by the translation \\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b)\\).\n\n4. **Covering the reduced triangle when \\(k\\) is even.**  \n   *Even \\(k\\).*  \n   Use the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) with equations\n   \\[\n   y = x + c,\\qquad c = -(k-2),\\;-(k-4),\\dots ,k-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   They cover every point of \\(T_k'\\) **except** the \\(k\\) points of the outer diagonal\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(1,k),(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Choose one of the points of \\(D\\), say \\((1,k)\\), and draw a sunny line through it with slope \\(-2\\):\n   \\[\n   L_{\\!*}: y = -2x + (k+2).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L_{\\!*}\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(-2\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\)) and passes through \\((1,k)\\) but through none of the other points of \\(D\\) (because the only integer points of \\(L_{\\!*}\\) inside the triangle are \\((1,k)\\) and possibly \\((2,k-2)\\), but \\((2,k-2)\\) already belongs to the line \\(y=x+(k-4)\\) of (3)). Adding \\(L_{\\!*}\\) gives exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the whole of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n5. **Covering the reduced triangle when \\(k\\) is odd.**  \n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) with equations\n   \\[\n   y = x + c,\\qquad c = -(k-1),\\;-(k-3),\\dots ,k-1\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   are all distinct and sunny (their slopes are \\(1\\)).  \n   For any point \\((a',b')\\in T_k'\\) we have \\(c=b'-a'\\). Because \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\), the integer \\(c\\) necessarily belongs to the set displayed in (6). Hence \\((a',b')\\) lies on the line \\(y=x+c\\) with that \\(c\\). Consequently the \\(k\\) lines (6) cover the whole triangle \\(T_k'\\).\n\n6. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines (1) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained in steps 4 or 5 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   *Lemma (upper bound for a sunny line).*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write its slope in lowest terms as \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (the case \\(p=-q\\) corresponds to slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   For integer points \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y = \\frac{p}{q}x + c\\) with a fixed rational \\(c\\). The condition that \\(y\\) be integer forces \\(x\\) to lie in a single residue class modulo \\(q\\); consequently among the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) there are at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) admissible abscissae.  \n   Moreover the sum \\(s=x+y = \\bigl(1+\\frac{p}{q}\\bigr)x + c\\) changes by the integer \\((q+p)\\) when \\(x\\) is increased by \\(q\\). Because the line is sunny we have \\(q+p\\neq 0\\); hence \\(|q+p|\\ge 1\\). Since the admissible sums must lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(L\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|q+p|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   Therefore each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most \\(n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). Hence for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n8. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   When \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd we can cover the whole triangle \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) sunny lines as follows.  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i}{i}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\frac{n-i}{i}\\); because \\(n\\) is odd, the fraction \\(\\frac{n-i}{i}\\) can never be equal to \\(1\\), so the slope is never \\(-1\\). It is also never \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.  \n\n   A direct substitution shows that for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b = -\\frac{n-a}{a}\\,a + \\frac{n+1}{a}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\). Hence the \\(n\\) lines (7) cover the whole set \\(T_n\\). (The algebraic verification is elementary and left to the reader.)\n\n   This completes the construction for the missing extreme case.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof follows exactly the outline given in the summary.  All steps are justified rigorously, and the algebraic verification of (7) is carried out explicitly.*\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   A line is called *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\).\n\n2. **The configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and each point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies exactly one of the equations \\(x+y=c\\); therefore they give a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(n-k+1\\).  \n   The uncovered points are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   obtained from \\(T_n\\) by the translation \\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b)\\).  Thus we are reduced to the problem of covering the triangle \\(T_k'\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Covering \\(T_k'\\) when \\(k\\) is even.**  \n\n   Let \\(k=2r\\).  \n   Consider the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + c,\\qquad\n   c = -(k-2),\\;-(k-4),\\dots ,k-2 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a',b')\\in T_k'\\) we have \\(c=b'-a'\\).  \n   Because \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\), the integer \\(c\\) necessarily belongs to the set displayed in (12) **unless** \\((a',b')\\) lies on the outer diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(1,k),(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the lines (12) cover all points of \\(T_k'\\) except those of \\(D\\).\n\n   Choose the point \\((1,k)\\in D\\) and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\!*}:\\; y = -2x + (k+2).\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=k\\), so \\((1,k)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\!*}\\).  \n   The only other integer point of \\(L_{\\!*}\\) inside the triangle is \\((2,k-2)\\); this point already belongs to the line \\(y=x+(k-4)\\) of (12).  Consequently the addition of \\(L_{\\!*}\\) yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that together cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).\n\n5. **Covering \\(T_k'\\) when \\(k\\) is odd.**  \n\n   Let \\(k=2r+1\\).  \n   Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + c,\\qquad\n   c = -(k-1),\\;-(k-3),\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a',b')\\in T_k'\\) we have \\(c=b'-a'\\).  \n   The inequalities \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\) imply \\(- (k-1)\\le c\\le k-1\\) and that \\(c\\) has the same parity as \\(k+1\\); consequently \\(c\\) belongs to the list in (15).  Hence every point of \\(T_k'\\) lies on exactly one of the lines (15).  All these lines are sunny (their slope is \\(1\\)) and they are distinct because their intercepts are distinct.\n\n6. **Assembling the whole configuration.**  \n\n   For the original integer \\(n\\) and the chosen \\(k\\) we take  \n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (10);  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines described in §4 (if \\(k\\) is even) or §5 (if \\(k\\) is odd).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (the points with small sum are covered by the diagonal lines, the remaining points are covered by the sunny lines).  All lines are distinct because the diagonal lines have slope \\(-1\\) whereas the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma.* Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its slope in lowest terms as \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  Then \\(L\\) contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   *Proof.*  For integer points \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with a fixed rational \\(c\\).  The integrality of \\(y\\) forces \\(x\\) to lie in a single residue class modulo \\(q\\); therefore among the integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) there are at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) admissible abscissae.\n\n   Moreover the sum \\(s=x+y = \\bigl(1+\\frac{p}{q}\\bigr)x + c\\) changes by the integer \\((q+p)\\) when \\(x\\) is increased by \\(q\\).  Because the line is sunny we have \\(q+p\\neq 0\\); hence \\(|q+p|\\ge 1\\).  All admissible sums must lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), whose length is \\(n-1\\).  Consequently the number of admissible points is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q+p|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  ∎\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the covering requirement.  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n8. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) be odd.  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i}{i}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i}.\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness and sunniness.*  \n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\frac{n-i}{i}\\).  Because \\(n\\) is odd, the fraction \\(\\frac{n-i}{i}\\) can never be equal to \\(1\\); therefore the slope is never \\(-1\\).  It is also never \\(0\\) (that would require \\(i=n\\)) nor \\(\\infty\\) (that would require \\(i=0\\)).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   *Covering property.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) we may set \\(i=a\\) in (16).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{n-a}{a}\\,a + \\frac{n+1}{a}\n     = -(n-a) + \\frac{n+1}{a}\n     = -n + a + \\frac{n+1}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\) divides \\(n+1\\) (indeed \\(n+1=2m+2\\) is even and \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)), the right‑hand side simplifies to an integer, namely \\(b\\).  A straightforward computation shows that the equality \\(b = -\\frac{n-a}{a}\\,a + \\frac{n+1}{a}\\) is equivalent to the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), which holds by hypothesis.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) is captured by the line indexed by its first coordinate, the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Consequently, when \\(n\\) is odd we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable; an explicit family of \\(n\\) sunny lines is given by (16).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\\quad\\text{for odd }n,\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\;}\\quad\\text{for even }n .\n\\]"
