take  \n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (10);  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines described in §4 (if \\(k\\) is even) or §5 (if \\(k\\) is odd).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (the points with small sum are covered by the diagonal lines, the remaining points are covered by the sunny lines).  All lines are distinct because the diagonal lines have slope \\(-1\\) whereas the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma.* Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its slope in lowest terms as \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  Then \\(L\\) contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   *Proof.*  For integer points \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with a fixed rational \\(c\\).  The integrality of \\(y\\) forces \\(x\\) to lie in a single residue class modulo \\(q\\); therefore among the integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) there are at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) admissible abscissae.\n\n   Moreover the sum \\(s=x+y = \\bigl(1+\\frac{p}{q}\\bigr)x + c\\) changes by the integer \\((q+p)\\) when \\(x\\) is increased by \\(q\\).  Because the line is sunny we have \\(q+p\\neq 0\\); hence \\(|q+p|\\ge 1\\).  All admissible sums must lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), whose length is \\(n-1\\).  Consequently the number of admissible points is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q+p|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  ∎\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the covering requirement.  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n8. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) be odd.  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i}{i}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i}.\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness and sunniness.*  \n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\frac{n-i}{i}\\).  Because \\(n\\) is odd, the fraction \\(\\frac{n-i}{i}\\) can never be equal to \\(1\\); therefore the slope is never \\(-1\\).  It is also never \\(0\\) (that would require \\(i=n\\)) nor \\(\\infty\\) (that would require \\(i=0\\)).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   *Covering property.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) we may set \\(i=a\\) in (16).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{n-a}{a}\\,a + \\frac{n+1}{a}\n     = -(n-a) + \\frac{n+1}{a}\n     = -n + a + \\frac{n+1}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\) divides \\(n+1\\) (indeed \\(n+1=2m+2\\) is even and \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)), the right‑hand side simplifies to an integer, namely \\(b\\).  A straightforward computation shows that the equality \\(b = -\\frac{n-a}{a}\\,a + \\frac{n+1}{a}\\) is equivalent to the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), which holds by hypothesis.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) is captured by the line indexed by its first coordinate, the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Consequently, when \\(n\\) is odd we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable; an explicit family of \\(n\\) sunny lines is given by (16).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\\quad\\text{for odd }n,\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\;}\\quad\\text{for even }n .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
