 n-1\\) ).**  \n   * Keep the first \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1\\).  \n   * The points that remain uncovered are exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     U_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\ge n-k+2\\}.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     (No translation is needed; (1) follows directly from the definition of the retained diagonals.)\n\n   * **Even \\(k\\).**  \n     Write \\(k=2r\\).  \n     Use the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n     \\[\n     L_c:\\;y=x+c,\\qquad c=-(k-2),-(k-4),\\dots ,k-2 .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     These lines cover every point of \\(U_{n,k}\\) except the \\(k\\) points  \n\n     \\[\n     D=\\{(1,k),(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\}.\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     (Verification: for a point \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(c=b-a\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the integer \\(c\\) belongs to the set in (2) unless \\((a,b)\\) is one of the points listed in (3).)\n\n     Add the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_*:\\;y=-2x+(k+2) .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     \\(L_*\\) passes through \\((1,k)\\) and through no other point of \\(D\\); the only other integer point of \\(L_*\\) inside the triangle is \\((2,k-2)\\), which already lies on the line \\(y=x+(k-4)\\) from (2).  \n     Consequently the family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) diagonals, the \\(k-1\\) lines (2) and the line (4) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   * **Odd \\(k\\).**  \n     Write \\(k=2r+1\\).  \n     Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n     \\[\n     L_c:\\;y=x+c,\\qquad c=-(k-1),-(k-3),\\dots ,k-1 .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     For any \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(c=b-a\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the integer \\(c\\) necessarily belongs to the set displayed in (5).  \n     Hence the \\(k\\) lines (5) cover the whole of \\(U_{n,k}\\).  \n     Together with the \\(n-k\\) retained diagonals we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines,