(\\lceil n/1\\rceil = n\\) points, but the sum‑step bound with \\(|p+q|=1\\) gives at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\rfloor+1 = n\\).  \n   A direct inspection of the equations \\(y=-2x+c\\) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+d\\) inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) shows that, when \\(n\\) is even, each of these lines contains **exactly** \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the points are obtained for the even abscissae in the first case and for the even abscissae in the second case).  \n   Hence in every case a sunny line contains at most \\(\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|\n   \\qquad(\\text{because }n\\ge 2).\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n7. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (so \\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n   *For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,m+1\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i^{(A)}:\\; y=-2x+(2i+1) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j^{(B)}:\\; y=-\\frac12x+(2m+2-j) .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines have slopes \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\); hence they are sunny and they are pairwise distinct because their intercepts are distinct.\n\n   **Claim.** The family \\(\\{\\ell_i^{(A)}\\}_{i=1}^{m+1}\\cup\\{\\ell_j^{(B)}\\}_{j=1}^{m}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   Exactly one of the numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is odd (because \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\) is even).  \n\n   *Case 1 – \\(a\\) is even.*  \n   Write \\(a=2u\\) with \\(1\\le u\\le m\\).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   c:=b+2a = b+4u .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we have \\(c\\le (2m+2-2u)+4u = 2m+2+2u\\le 2m+2+2m = 4m+2 = 2n\\).  \n   Moreover \\(c\\ge 3\\).  \n   The even numbers that occur as \\(c\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   2m+3,\\,2m+5,\\dots ,2n-1 = 4m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are exactly the intercepts of the lines \\(\\ell_i^{(A)}\\) (formula (7) with \\(i=m+1-u\\)).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{\\,m+1-u}^{(A)}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(a\\) is odd.*  \n   Write \\(a=2u+1\\) with \\(0\\le u\\le m\\).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b+\\frac{a}{2}=b+u+\\tfrac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(b\\) is an integer, \\(d\\) is a half‑integer.  \n   Multiplying by two we obtain the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   2d = 2b + a = (a+b) + (b-a) .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   2d\\in\\{\\,2m+3,\\,2m+5,\\dots ,4m+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   d\\in\\Bigl\\{\\,m+\\tfrac32,\\;m+\\tfrac52,\\dots ,2m+\\tfrac12\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers on the right‑hand side are exactly the intercepts appearing in (8) (the line \\(\\ell_j^{(B)}\\) has intercept \\(2m+2-j = m+\\tfrac12 + (m+ \\tfrac12 -j)\\)).  \n   Choosing \\(j=m-u\\) we obtain \\(d\\) as the intercept of \\(\\ell_{\\,m-u}^{(B)}\\), and therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on that line.\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to one of the constructed lines, proving the claim. ∎\n\n   Hence for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with **all \\(n\\) lines sunny**, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (construction in §5).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Lemma in §6).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists (construction in §7).\n\nTherefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for all }n\\ge3,\n\\qquad\\text{and also }k=n\\text{ when }n\\text{ 