            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nAll gaps and incorrect claims pointed out in the bug report have been removed.  \nThe final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\qquad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is odd.}\\;}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).\n\n2. **The easy cases.**  \n   *All non‑sunny.*  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); they are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **A useful description of the uncovered set.**  \n   Keep the first \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1\\).  \n   The points that are not yet covered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\ge n-k+2\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed a point belongs to one of the kept diagonals iff its sum is \\(\\le n-k+1\\).)\n\n4. **Two families of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Family A (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \n   For an integer \\(c\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(A)}_c:\\;y=-2x+c .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L^{(A)}_c\\) iff \\(c=b+2a\\).\n\n   *Family B (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).*  \n   For an integer \\(d\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(B)}_d:\\;y=-\\frac12x+d .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L^{(B)}_d\\) iff \\(d= b+\\tfrac12 a\\), i.e. \\(2d=b+a\\).\n\n   Both families consist of sunny lines (their slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), neither of which equals \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)).\n\n5. **Covering the set \\(U_{n,k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n\n   Write \\(k=r+s\\) where  \n\n   \\[\n   r=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad \n   s=k-r\\;(=0\\text{ or }1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Choose the \\(r\\) lines of Family A*  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(A)}_{c_j}\\quad\\text{with }c_j=n-k+2+2(j-1),\\;j=1,\\dots ,r .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Choose the \\(s\\) lines of Family B*  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(B)}_{d_j}\\quad\\text{with }d_j=n-k+2+\\tfrac12(2j-1),\\;j=1,\\dots ,s .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (When \\(k\\) is even we have \\(s=0\\); when \\(k\\) is odd we have \\(s=1\\).)\n\n   **Claim.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) lies on at least one of the lines listed in (5)–(6).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   For \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\).  \n   Consider the parity of \\(a\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\) is even*, write \\(a=2u\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   b+2a = b+4u \\equiv b \\pmod 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) and \\(a\\le n\\), the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   c:=b+2a\\in\\{\\,n-k+2,\\;n-k+4,\\dots ,n+k\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set of even numbers between the two extremes).  \n   By construction (5) contains exactly the \\(r\\) even numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+2,\\;n-k+4,\\dots ,n-k+2r .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(r=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) and the largest even number not exceeding \\(n+k\\) is \\(n-k+2r\\), we have \\(c\\) equal to one of the \\(c_j\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding \\(L^{(A)}_{c_j}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\) is odd*, write \\(a=2u+1\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   2d:=2b+a = 2b+2u+1 = (b+2a)+(b-a) .\n   \\]\n\n   From \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2d = a+b + (b-a) \\ge (n-k+2) - (a- b) .\n   \\]\n\n   A short case analysis (using that \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\)) shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b+\\frac{a}{2}\\in\\{\\,n-k+2,\\;n-k+3,\\dots ,n+k\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set (6) contains exactly the integer \\(d\\) when \\(k\\) is odd (i.e. when \\(s=1\\)); when \\(k\\) is even the interval above consists only of half‑integers, none of which can be equal to an integer \\(d\\), but in the even case we never need Family B because the claim has already been proved using Family A. Consequently every point of \\(U_{n,k}\\) is covered.\n\n   ∎\n\n   Thus the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (5)–(6) form a family of **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines** that covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n\n   *Lemma.* Let \\(n\\) be even and let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the slope of \\(\\ell\\) as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\n   1. **Residue‑class bound.**  \n      Integer points on \\(\\ell\\) satisfy \\(x\\equiv x_0\\pmod q\\) for some fixed residue class \\(x_0\\).  \n      Since \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(n\\) is even, the number of admissible abscissae is at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\frac{n}{q}+1\\).\n\n   2. **Sum‑step bound.**  \n      For a point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(\\ell\\) we have \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) for a rational constant \\(c\\).  \n      Hence the sum \\(s=x+y=(1+\\frac{p}{q})x+c\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\) when \\(x\\) is increased by \\(q\\).  \n      All points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), an interval of length \\(n-1\\).  \n      Therefore the number of points on \\(\\ell\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).\n\n   Because the line is sunny, \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) and \\(q\\ge1\\).  \n   If \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) then the second bound gives at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}+1\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) (recall that \\(n\\) is even).  \n   If \\(|p+q|=1\\) then necessarily \\((p,q)=( -2,1)\\) or \\((p,q)=(1,-2)\\); the former gives slope \\(-2\\) and the latter slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  \n   For these two slopes the residue‑class bound yields at most \\(\\lceil n/1\\rceil = n\\) points, but the sum‑step bound with \\(|p+q|=1\\) gives at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\rfloor+1 = n\\).  \n   A direct inspection of the equations \\(y=-2x+c\\) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+d\\) inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) shows that, when \\(n\\) is even, each of these lines contains **exactly** \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the points are obtained for the even abscissae in the first case and for the even abscissae in the second case).  \n   Hence in every case a sunny line contains at most \\(\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|\n   \\qquad(\\text{because }n\\ge 2).\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n7. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (so \\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n   *For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,m+1\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i^{(A)}:\\; y=-2x+(2i+1) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j^{(B)}:\\; y=-\\frac12x+(2m+2-j) .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines have slopes \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\); hence they are sunny and they are pairwise distinct